# Edward McPherson Farm: Historical Study (Kathleen R. Georg, Research Historian, Gettysburg National Military Park; October 14, 1977): the farm in 1863 (Slentz tenancy), the fighting of July 1 and the use of the farm buildings by the wounded.
# A typescript whose text layer has letter-spacing noise and a few wrong letters (bam for barn, digit misreads such as 'lit9th' for 149th); every quote is typed in the cleaned form (letters and digits match, spacing ignored) with the explicit {{saved|fixed}} mark where the text layer is wrong.
# PDF pages 23, 28, 43, 48, 53, 73 and 80 were looked at as page images; the printed page numbers (21, 26, 41, 46, 51, 71, 78) were read from them.
# The study quotes the claims file of the tenant John T. Slentz, regimental histories and accounts of participants and civilians: each record names the basis the page itself gives.
@ NPS-HS-MCPHERSON
%images 23, 28, 43, 48, 53, 73, 80

%basis Claims file of John T. Slentz, tenant (the study's footnote 41)
23/21 | fields | The McPherson farm at the planting of 1863: over half of the 95 acres in wheat, corn, oats and grass; about thirty acres of woodland, pasture and a young orchard | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-06 | stated | The study says that over half of the 95 acres was planted in crops, wheat, corn, oats and grass, and that the remainder, about thirty acres, was woodland and pasture for the stock and a young orchard for home preserving and cooking, the situation of the tenant John Slentz at the planting of 1863 | =Over half of the 95 acres was planted in crops--wheat, corn, oats, and grass. The remainder, about thirty acres, was in woodland and pasture for the stock and a young orchard for home preserving and cooking use. | =by 1863's planting {{timg|time}} he had three cows and six calves, four horses, four hogs, three turkeys, and at least forty chickens.
23/21 | general | The tenant's stock in June 1863: three cows and six calves, four horses, four hogs, three turkeys, forty chickens | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-06 | stated | The study says that by the planting of 1863 the tenant John Slentz had three cows and six calves, four horses, four hogs, three turkeys and at least forty chickens | =by 1863's planting {{timg|time}} he had three cows and six calves, four horses, four hogs, three turkeys, and at least forty chickens.
%basis Sarah Slentz's recollection about seventy years later of the farmhouse of her childhood (the study's footnote 38: Sheads and Dunn, Historian's Report, Old McPherson Farm House)
21/- | buildings | The McPherson farmhouse plan: a log back part with kitchen and dining room, a stone front part with bedroom and parlor, two bedrooms above each, a two-step difference between the parts | bld-mcpherson-farm | ~1860s (the house of the early 1860s as a child remembered it about 1930; the study says the 1863 Brady photograph verifies log and stone) | stated | The study gives Sarah Slentz's account that in the back part of the house, built of logs, were a kitchen and dining room downstairs and two bedrooms above, in the front part, built of stone, a bedroom and a living room or parlor downstairs and two bedrooms above, with a two-step elevation between the parts both upstairs and downstairs | =In the back part downstairs (built of logs) were a kitchen and dining room. In the front part downstairs (built of stone) were a bedroom and a living room or parlor. On the second floor of the back log section were two bedrooms. On the second floor of the front stone section were another two bedrooms. According to Sarah Slentz, when you traversed the building from the back to the front (i.e., the log to the stone), you had to make a two-step elevation both upstairs and downstairs.
%basis Slentz claims (the study's footnote 49)
28/26 | damage | On the evening of June 30 horses trampled the oats and wheat and soldiers tore down the tenant's fencing for firewood and possible breastworks | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-06-30 | stated | The study says that on the night of Tuesday June 30 the tenant watched men and their horses trample his oats and wheat and tear down his fencing for firewood and for possible use as breastworks on the morrow | =He watched helplessly as men and their horses trampled oats and wheat, tore down his fencing for firewood and for possible use as breastworks on the morrow, and looked on his turkeys, chickens, hogs, and cows with ravenous eyes.
28/26 | fences | Buford's troopers cut and threw down about a quarter mile of post and rail fence on the McPherson farm and used another cord of rails for firewood | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-06-30 | stated | The study says that Buford's troopers cut and threw down about a quarter mile of post and rail fence to make way for the battle and used up another cord of rails for firewood, as the tenant later claimed | =After watching Buford's troopers cut and throw down about a quarter mile of post and rail fence "to make way for the battle" and another cord of rails used up for firewood,
%basis the regimental histories of Chamberlin (1905), Bassler (1909) and Hubler (1913) cited in the study's footnotes 71-73
48/46 | orchards | July 1: the 150th Pennsylvania in the orchard south of the McPherson barn, the 149th in the lane fronting the barnyard, the 143rd near the stone barn | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07-01 | stated | The study says that Stone's Brigade was deployed with the 150th Pennsylvania in the orchard south of the McPherson barn, the 149th lying in the lane fronting the barnyard, and the 143rd behind them with its right near the stone barn and its left toward Herbst Woods | =This brigade had been deployed so that the 150th Pennsylvania was slightly to the left and front of the {{ll;9th|149th}} Pennsylvania, in the orchard south of the McPherson barn, while the {{ll;9th|149th}} lay in the lane fronting the barnyard with part of its right flank facing northward at right angles, south of the Cashtown Pike. The {{ll;3rd|143rd}} Pennsylvania was behind these two, with its right near the stone barn and its left extending southerly in the direction of Herbst Woods.
%basis -
53/51 | fences | Rails stacked as breastworks by Buford's troopers, taken from a fence on the eastern edge of a wheatfield between the railroad grading and the turnpike | - | 1863-07-01 | stated | The study says a squad of the 149th Pennsylvania took advantage of a pile of rails stacked as breastworks by Buford's troopers, which were carried from a fence bounding the eastern edge of a wheatfield that sloped westward down the ridge between the railroad grading and the turnpike | =Here the squad took advantage of a pile of rails stacked as breastworks by Buford's troopers. The rails were carried from a fence bounding the eastern edge of a wheatfield which sloped westward down the ridge between the railroad grading and the turnpike.
43/41 | fields | A high wheatfield north of the railroad grading on July 1 | - | 1863-07-01 | stated | The study says that men of Davis's captured brigade escaped through the railroad cut toward the Confederate lines through a high wheatfield north of the railroad grading | =through a high wheatfield north of the railroad grading.
63/61 | fields | Confederates crawled on hands and knees through the high wheat to surprise a color guard | - | 1863-07-01 | stated | The study says a squad of Confederates crawled on their hands and knees through the high wheat to escape detection and surprised the color guard of a Pennsylvania regiment | =These Confederates had crawled on their hands and knees through the high wheat to escape detection,
57/55 | fences | A post and rail fence paralleling the south side of the Cashtown Pike | - | 1863-07-01 | stated | The study says Pennsylvania companies lay behind the post and rail fence paralleling the south side of the pike | =behind the post and rail fence paralleling the south side of the pike.
67/65 | fields | The cornfields and orchard of the McPherson acreage | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07-01 | stated | The study says that when the last of Stone's Brigade had retreated toward the seminary the McPherson acreage saw no more hostilities over its cornfields and orchard | =When the last of Stone's Brigade melted back towards the seminary, and eventually into town and the Cemetery heights, McPherson's acreage saw no more hostilities over its cornfields and orchard.
33/31 | woodlots | Herbst Woods, the woodlot of the 160-acre Herbst farm, was wholly Herbst's; the McPherson woodlot lay north of the Cashtown Pike east of the Wills farm | bld-herbst | 1863-07 | stated | The study says the entire length and width of the grove called Reynolds Woods or McPherson Woods was owned by John Herbst at the time of the battle and was the woodlot portion of his 160-acre farm, that the McPherson woodlot was north of the Cashtown Pike and east of the James Wills farm, and that Edward McPherson never owned the Herbst Woods | =But the fact remains that the entire length and width of the grove was owned by John Herbst at the time of the Battle of Gettysburg, and was the woodlot portion of the 160-acre Herbst Farm. The McPherson woodlot, at that time, was north of the Cashtown Pike and east of the James Wills Farm (in the area now erroneously called Sheads' Woods). The Herbst Woods were never owned by Edward McPherson, John B. McPherson, or any other McPherson from the time antecedent to the battle to the time after the battle.
33/31 | buildings | The Herbst barn was burned because Union soldiers sniped from his buildings; the McPherson buildings were not lost | bld-herbst,bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07-01 | stated | The study says John Herbst had his barn burned because Union soldiers used his buildings to snipe at Heth's men, and that McPherson lost no buildings | =Poor John Herbst had his barn burned because Yankee soldiers used his buildings from which to snipe at Heth's men. (McPherson lost no buildings.)
70/- | woodlots | A large white oak just north of Herbst Woods and the old fence line, about one third its present size in 1863 | bld-herbst | 1863-07-01 (the tree as the study reconstructs its size) | hedged | The study says a large white oak just north of Herbst Woods and the old fence line, with a circumference of over nine feet in the 1970s and an age of about 170 years, would have been about one third of that size in 1863 | =A large white oak, just north of Herbst Woods and the old fence line, stands at fifty yards east of the 7th Wisconsin monument. This tree, with a circumference of over nine feet now, is approximately 170 years old, and is apparently the only old tree in this part of the field north of the woods. It would have been about one-third that size in 1863
%basis Lieutenant Colonel J. A. Walker, 45th Georgia, "Some Stirring Incidents", Philadelphia Times, March 17, 188-, quoted by the study (footnote 105)
73/71 | fences | Wounded sitting about the fence corners; the fence at the corner of Reynolds woods where it turns toward Herr's Tavern | - | 1863-07-01 (evening; an account of the 1880s) | stated | The study quotes a Confederate officer's account that after burying many in the field he came about sundown to the corner of the Reynolds woods and rested on a fence very near the corner where it turns toward Herr's Tavern, and that a few wounded sat about the fence corners | =I first came upon the ground fought over by McGowan's South Carolina troops. Many of his dead were still unburied and a few wounded sitting about the fence corners. . . . We buried a great many in this field and about sundown came to the corner of the Reynolds woods, where General Reynolds had been killed that day. I rested for a while on this fence, very near the corner where it turns towards Herr's Tavern.
%basis -
73/71 | hospitals | July 1: the ambulatory wounded sought refuge in the McPherson farmhouse and barn; the fields strewn with the dead and wounded | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07-01 | stated | The study says that the wounded who were ambulatory enough sought refuge in the McPherson farmhouse and barn and that at dusk on July 1 the fields around the farm were strewn with the dead, dying and wounded of both armies | =Those wounded who were ambulatory enough to make it, sought refuge in the McPherson farmhouse and barn, while those less fortunate in the serious nature of their wounds had to rely on the devotion and bravery of friends to assist them in reaching even the crudest of shelter (such as in Major Chamberlin's case). As dusk closed in on July 1, however, the fields around the McPherson Farm were strewn with the dead, dying, and wounded men of both armies,
%basis Lieutenant Colonel J. A. Walker, 45th Georgia, as quoted by the study (footnote 105)
74/72 | hospitals | The tenant house, taken and retaken, riddled and filled with dead and wounded Federals but not materially damaged | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07-01 | stated | The study quotes a Confederate officer's account that the farm house, a few hundred yards north of the Reynolds oak, was taken and retaken, riddled by bullets and filled with the dead and dying, the cows and horses shot down by stray bullets, and yet not materially damaged, and that he found it filled with Federal dead and wounded | =Taken and re-taken, riddled by bullets, filled with dead and dying, the very cows and horses shot down by stray bullets, and yet not materially damaged. Seeing a light in it I went to see if my services were needed, I found it filled with Federal dead and wounded.
%basis Lieutenant R. B. Beath, 88th Pennsylvania, as quoted by the study
76/74 | hospitals | The McPherson barn full of bleeding and mangled soldiers without a surgeon after July 1 | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07-01 | stated | The study quotes a Union lieutenant's account that on reaching McPherson's barn he found it full of bleeding and mangled soldiers in a most distressed and sickening condition, without a surgeon to dress their festering wounds and bind up their splintered bones | =upon reaching McPherson's {{bam|barn}} he found it full of bleeding and mangled soldiers in a most distressed and sickening condition, without a surgeon to dress their festering wounds and bind up their splintered bones.
%basis -
78/76 | hospitals | After the armies withdrew the wounded in and about the McPherson farm buildings were unattended until citizens came | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07-04 to 1863-07-05 | stated | The study says that after the withdrawal of the armies the soldiers still lay in and about McPherson's farm buildings unattended, that it was not until the Confederate forces melted back toward the Potomac that those in the dark barn saw hope, and that the first to relieve them were concerned citizens, not army or civil surgeons | =After the withdrawal of the armies starting on July {{5>|5}}, these soldiers still lay in and about McPherson's farm buildings unattended. | =It was not until after the Confederate forces melted back towards the Potomac that those lying within the dark {{bam|barn}} saw any rays of hope. The first to relieve the wants of these melancholy soldiers were not army or civil surgeons, but concerned citizens from the area.
%basis a civilian's account (probably William McClean, "The Days of Terror in 1863") as quoted by the study (footnote 110)
79/77 | hospitals | The McPherson barn crowded with the wounded of both armies, some four days without food or surgical attention | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07-05 | stated | The study quotes a civilian's account that on entering the barn on the Chambersburg Pike he found it crowded with the wounded of both armies, some having fallen four days before, without food or surgical attention, so closely packed that he had to tramp on some of them while handing out supplies | =When I entered the barn it was crowded with the wounded of both armies, some of them having fallen four days before and without having any food, except in some cases the little hardtack in their haversacks, and without any surgical attention to their wounds. There were so many of these wounded and so closely packed together, that I was obliged to tramp on some of them in distributing my supplies.
%basis a chaplain's account of July 5 quoted by the study
80/78 | hospitals | July 5: the barn above and below, the wagon shed, the tenant house, the pig sty and the barnyard crowded with badly wounded soldiers | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07-05 | stated | The study quotes a chaplain's account of July 5 that the barn above and below, the wagon shed, the tenant house, the pig sty and the open barnyard were all crowded with badly wounded soldiers, and that not until about noon did part of the hospital corps arrive, only one surgeon and two assistants | =When I examined the situation I found that the barn above and below, the wagon shed, the tenant house, the pig sty, and the open barnyard were all crowded with badly wounded soldiers. | As time passed, other persons strolled in and rendered aid, but it was not until about noon that any part of the hospital corps arrived. And then only one surgeon and two assistants came to render their service.
